{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. outcomes (often by ~1–3 EUR early, widening to ~5–12 EUR at longer horizons during selloffs). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, larger when recent trend is down.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the last 2–4 weeks show a clear decline, the model path falls too slowly and then stays elevated. Add extra negative drift proportional to the recent 10–20 day slope (stronger correction beyond ~day 10).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with horizon (especially days 15–30). Use a ramping correction: small adjustment days 1–5, moderate days 6–15, strongest days 16–30.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: model frequently predicts rebounds/mean reversion to low-70s/ mid-70s that do not materialize; realized prices continue lower or stay depressed. Damp rebound amplitude (shrink positive moves) and cap upside relative to last price during bearish regimes.",
    "Regime switch heuristic: if last price is below the 20-day average and the 20-day average is falling, treat as bearish regime—apply stronger downward correction and reduce volatility/oscillation in the forecast (smoother, more monotone decline)."
  ]
}
